The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and the statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), in particular resolutions 2666 (2022), 2717 (2023), 2746 (2024), 2765 (2024), 2773 (2025), and 2783 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity, and territorial integrity of the DRC as well as all States in the region, and emphasizing the urgent need to respect fully the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness, and regional cooperation,

Reiterating its deep concern at the continuing deterioration of the security, human rights, and humanitarian situation in eastern DRC, particularly in North Kivu, South Kivu, and Ituri, driven by the persistent violence perpetrated by armed groups, including the March 23 Movement (M23), the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR), the Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), the Coopérative pour le développement du Congo (CODECO), and various local armed groups,

Condemning in the strongest terms the military offensives, territorial expansion, and abuses committed by the M23, and reiterating that the M23 and all other armed groups must immediately cease all hostilities, permanently withdraw from all occupied areas, and disarm unconditionally in accordance with the decisions taken within regional and international frameworks,

Reiterating its condemnation of external support to armed groups active in the DRC, strongly condemning the continued unauthorized presence and military operations of Rwandan forces on the territory of the DRC as documented by the Group of Experts, and demanding the immediate, unconditional, and complete withdrawal of all Rwandan Defence Forces from Congolese territory,

Demanding equally that the Government of the DRC cease any operational support to or collaboration with the FDLR, and stressing the imperative of the complete neutralization and disarmament of the FDLR,

Welcoming the peace agreement signed in Washington, D.C., on 27 June 2025 between the DRC and Rwanda under the facilitation of the United States, as well as the ongoing efforts through the Luanda and Nairobi processes, and urging both parties to fully, swiftly, and in good faith implement their commitments, including the operationalization of the joint security cooperation mechanism and the verified withdrawal of external forces,

Expressing grave concern over the unprecedented humanitarian catastrophe affecting millions of civilians, marked by acute food insecurity, mass internal displacement, and widespread human rights violations, including horrific levels of conflict-related sexual violence and serious violations against children, and demanding that all parties ensure rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access,

Stressing the primary responsibility of the Government of the DRC to protect civilians within its territory and to hold accountable all perpetrators of violations of international humanitarian law and violations and abuses of human rights,

Emphasizing the vital role played by the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) in the protection of civilians, support to the stabilization of the country, and facilitation of humanitarian assistance, and paying tribute to the sacrifice and dedication of MONUSCO peacekeepers operating under increasingly challenging and hostile conditions,

Reiterating its condemnation of all attacks, threats, harassment, disinformation, and obstruction directed against MONUSCO personnel, premises, and assets, including the use of surface-to-air missiles and GPS jamming systems by armed elements and external forces, which directly endanger peacekeepers, humanitarian flights, and civilian aviation,

Taking note of the Report of the Secretary-General on MONUSCO (S/2025/779),

Determining that the situation in the DRC continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MONUSCO, including its Intervention Brigade, until 20 December 2026;

2. Decides to maintain the authorized troop ceiling of MONUSCO at 13,500 military personnel, 660 military observers and staff officers, 591 police personnel, and 1,050 personnel of formed police units, and requests the Secretary-General to ensure the mission maintains maximum operational flexibility, mobility, and readiness to protect civilians;

3. Authorizes MONUSCO to pursue the following strategic priorities:
(a) Protection of civilians, through proactive, robust, and mobile deployment, preventive posture, and armed intervention when civilians are under imminent threat, in accordance with the basic principles of peacekeeping and international humanitarian law;
(b) Support to the stabilization and strengthening of State institutions, including security sector reform, judicial capacity, and the disarmament, demobilization, community recovery and stabilization programme (P-DDRCS);
(c) Support to regional peace efforts, including the implementation of the 27 June 2025 Washington Agreement and the Luanda and Nairobi processes, through technical, logistical, and political facilitation;

4. Reaffirms that the withdrawal of MONUSCO must be gradual, responsible, conditions-based, and informed by the security situation on the ground, so as not to create security vacuums that further endanger civilian populations;

5. Notes the completion of the first phase of the disengagement plan in South Kivu, but underlines that any further stages of disengagement, particularly in North Kivu and Ituri, must be calibrated in close coordination with the Government of the DRC and assessed against concrete progress in the restoration of State authority and the protection of civilians;

6. Demands that all armed groups, notably the M23, FDLR, ADF, and CODECO, immediately cease all forms of violence, permanently lay down their arms, and release all abducted civilians, including children;

7. Calls upon all States in the region to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of their neighbours, demands the immediate and complete cessation of all external support, logistical assistance, and sanctuary provided to the M23 and any other armed group operating in the DRC, and reiterates its demand for the prompt and verified withdrawal of all Rwandan military forces from the territory of the DRC;

8. Urges the Governments of the DRC and Rwanda to fully implement the commitments undertaken under the Washington Agreement of 27 June 2025, in full coordination with the Luanda Process, including the rapid operationalization of the joint security mechanism to oversee the withdrawal of external forces and the cessation of support to and neutralization of the FDLR;

9. Demands that all parties respect the freedom of movement and access of MONUSCO, unreservedly cease any interference with the Mission's air and ground operations, including the use of electronic warfare, GPS jamming, and anti-aircraft weaponry, and emphasizes that any attack on UN peacekeepers may constitute a war crime;

10. Strongly condemns the illegal exploitation and illicit trade of natural resources in the DRC, which fuel and finance the conflict, reminds all actors that illicit trafficking in minerals is subject to targeted sanctions, and urges all Member States to implement effective supply chain due diligence;

11. Demands that all parties to the conflict allow and facilitate the full, safe, rapid, and unhindered passage of humanitarian relief for all civilians in need, and respect the civilian character of displacement camps and humanitarian infrastructure;

12. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council every 90 days on the implementation of MONUSCO's mandate, the security and humanitarian situation in the DRC, and progress in regional political agreements and the Mission’s gradual disengagement;

13. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

